The parent array stores the representative of each connected group. A successful merge reduces the number of groups. A connection whose endpoints already have the same representative is a spare cable.

Only `groups - 1` spare cables are needed. The edge count check covers cases where there are not enough cables at all.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int solve(int n, List<int[]> Edge) {
        if (Edge.Count < n - 1) {
            return -1;
        }

        int[] parent = new int[n];
        int[] rank = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            parent[i] = i;
        }

        int groups = n;
        int spare = 0;

        foreach (int[] edge in Edge) {
            int first = Find(edge[0], parent);
            int second = Find(edge[1], parent);

            if (first == second) {
                spare++;
                continue;
            }

            if (rank[first] < rank[second]) {
                parent[first] = second;
            } else if (rank[first] > rank[second]) {
                parent[second] = first;
            } else {
                parent[second] = first;
                rank[first]++;
            }

            groups--;
        }

        int needed = groups - 1;
        return spare >= needed ? needed : -1;
    }

    private int Find(int node, int[] parent) {
        if (parent[node] != node) {
            parent[node] = Find(parent[node], parent);
        }
        return parent[node];
    }
}
```

An empty edge list is valid when `n = 1`, because there is already one connected network. For more computers, the early return rejects an edge list shorter than `n - 1`.

Time complexity: `O(e * alpha(n))`, nearly linear in the number of connections.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the parent and rank arrays.

A graph component is a group of computers that can already reach each other. After counting these groups, only `components - 1` cable moves are needed to join them.

There must be at least `n - 1` connections first. If not, even a perfect arrangement cannot connect all computers.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
    void dfs(int node, vector<vector<int>> &graph, vector<bool> &visited) {
        visited[node] = true;
        for (int next : graph[node]) {
            if (!visited[next]) {
                dfs(next, graph, visited);
            }
        }
    }

public:
    int solve(int n, vector<vector<int>> &Edge){
        if ((int)Edge.size() < n - 1) {
            return -1;
        }

        vector<vector<int>> graph(n);
        for (const auto &edge : Edge) {
            graph[edge[0]].push_back(edge[1]);
            graph[edge[1]].push_back(edge[0]);
        }

        vector<bool> visited(n, false);
        int components = 0;

        for (int node = 0; node < n; node++) {
            if (!visited[node]) {
                components++;
                dfs(node, graph, visited);
            }
        }

        return components - 1;
    }
};
```

For example, with four computers and edges `(0,1)`, `(1,2)`, and `(2,0)`, DFS finds two components: `{0,1,2}` and `{3}`. The answer is `2 - 1 = 1`.

Time complexity: `O(n + e)`, where `e` is the number of connections.
Space complexity: `O(n + e)` for the graph and visited array. The input edge list is not changed.

A stack-based graph walk avoids recursive calls. Every time the outer loop finds an unvisited computer, it starts a new walk and increases the component count.

If the number of cables is too small, the result is immediately `-1`. Otherwise, connecting all found groups takes one fewer operation than the number of groups.

```go
func solve(n int, edges [][]int) int {
	if len(edges) < n-1 {
		return -1
	}

	graph := make([][]int, n)
	for _, edge := range edges {
		u, v := edge[0], edge[1]
		graph[u] = append(graph[u], v)
		graph[v] = append(graph[v], u)
	}

	visited := make([]bool, n)
	stack := make([]int, 0, n)
	components := 0

	for start := 0; start < n; start++ {
		if visited[start] {
			continue
		}

		components++
		visited[start] = true
		stack = append(stack, start)

		for len(stack) > 0 {
			last := len(stack) - 1
			node := stack[last]
			stack = stack[:last]

			for _, next := range graph[node] {
				if !visited[next] {
					visited[next] = true
					stack = append(stack, next)
				}
			}
		}
	}

	return components - 1
}
```

For `n = 3` and edges `(0,1)` and `(1,2)`, the stack walk visits all three computers from `0`, so there is one component and the answer is `0`.

Time complexity: `O(n + e)`.
Space complexity: `O(n + e)` for the adjacency lists, stack, and visited slice. No edge is removed from the input.

This version uses a queue instead of recursion. Each BFS starts from one computer that has not been visited yet, so it counts one separate network.

The early edge check handles the impossible case. A network with `n` computers needs at least `n - 1` cables.

```java
class Solution {
    public int solve(int n, int[][] Edge) {
        if (Edge.length < n - 1) {
            return -1;
        }

        int[][] graph = new int[n][n];
        int[] degree = new int[n];

        for (int[] edge : Edge) {
            int u = edge[0];
            int v = edge[1];
            graph[u][degree[u]++] = v;
            graph[v][degree[v]++] = u;
        }

        boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];
        int[] queue = new int[n];
        int components = 0;

        for (int start = 0; start < n; start++) {
            if (visited[start]) {
                continue;
            }

            components++;
            int front = 0;
            int back = 0;
            queue[back++] = start;
            visited[start] = true;

            while (front < back) {
                int node = queue[front++];
                for (int i = 0; i < degree[node]; i++) {
                    int next = graph[node][i];
                    if (!visited[next]) {
                        visited[next] = true;
                        queue[back++] = next;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return components - 1;
    }
}
```

A single computer is also a component, so `n = 1` with no edges returns `0`. The matrix keeps the code simple, but it uses more memory than a normal adjacency list.

Time complexity: `O(n^2 + e)` because the adjacency matrix is scanned through each computer's stored neighbors.
Space complexity: `O(n^2)` for the matrix and `O(n)` for the queue and visited array.
